# S2, shader bank family A: shapes, glows, gradients, easing, light (12 October 2026)

The family A harvest adds 35 blocks to the shader bank. Each one is a pure GLSL ES 3.0 function with a numpy twin of the same formula. All 35 pass `Scripts/VerifyShaderBank.py` at the defaults and at 2 random sets, and again at 6 random sets: the worst mean error is 0.34 levels and the worst maximum is 0.502 levels, which is 8-bit rounding and nothing more. (12 October 2026)

## Provenance and licences (12 October 2026)

None of the 35 blocks copies third-party code. They are all written from the mathematics: the box and segment distances, the polynomial smooth minimum, the sector folding of polygons, Schlick fresnel, bias and gain, and Blinn-Phong. Their `licence` field starts with `original`. (12 October 2026)

The two OKLab blocks (`ramp_oklab4` and `radial_oklab2`) use the numeric matrices published by Bjorn Ottosson in 2020. Their `source_url` points to https://bottosson.github.io/posts/oklab/. The matrices are numeric constants and no code was copied. (12 October 2026)

No Shadertoy code, no LYGIA code and no pixel, logo or text from a reference was used. (12 October 2026)

## Generator, the bank's HDA definition (12 October 2026)

`/Users/zenray/.claude/tmp/tmp-zenray-motion-design/ZenRayMotionDesign/Scripts/HarvestShapesLight.py` writes every block.

- **Helpers:** the GLSL helpers (`cov`, `box`, `axis`, `smin`, `srgb`, `oklab`, `ramp3`) are written once and copied into each block with the `<id>_` prefix. The numpy helpers are copied once into each twin, so every twin stays a standalone file.
- **No overwriting:** the script never overwrites an existing folder. `--force` rewrites only the folders that carry its own marker (`generated by Scripts/HarvestShapesLight.py`), and `--only a,b` limits a run to the listed blocks.
- **Index:** `index.json` is rebuilt from the folders by `bank.rebuild_index()` while holding an `fcntl` lock on `datasets/shaders/.index.lock`. A concurrent writer is never lost, because the index is always rebuilt from all the folders on disk.

SHA-256 of the script: `0cef9db8e7fa4d6efd8bc3483c75f87baf6837d03d5eb09872b5a3fec0f280a9`. (12 October 2026)

## The 35 blocks (12 October 2026)

| Family | Blocks |
|---|---|
| mask (12) | `sdf_circle`, `sdf_ring`, `sdf_capsule`, `sdf_superellipse`, `sdf_arch`, `sdf_parabola`, `sdf_ngon`, `sdf_smooth_union`, `sdf_smooth_subtract`, `sdf_onion_rect`, `sdf_concentric_rings`, `sdf_arc` |
| glow (6), colour, for add or screen | `glow_inverse_point`, `glow_gaussian`, `glow_ring`, `glow_rounded_rect`, `glow_segment`, `glow_parabola` |
| gradient (6) | `radial_gradient3`, `conic_gradient3` (seamless, cyclic), `ramp_oklab4`, `ramp_linear_light2` (gamma-correct), `radial_oklab2`, `bilinear_gradient4` |
| curve (4) | `ease_smootherstep_band` (mask), `ease_power_ramp`, `ease_expo_ramp`, `ease_bias_gain_ramp` |
| light (7) | `fresnel_rim_disc`, `rim_light_rect` (light direction taken from the analytic SDF gradient), `metal_ring_tint` (PBR-ish metal: Lambert plus Blinn specular, with Schlick F0 set to the metal tint), `light_sweep`, `specular_band_curved`, `vignette_radial`, `vignette_rounded_rect` (both used with the multiply blend) |

**Parameter conventions:**

- Every block that needs to stay isotropic takes `aspect` (canvas width / height) and works in height units.
- Angles are in degrees, with 0 pointing right and 90 pointing down.
- Random ranges are bounded so that the shapes stay visible.
- `smoothstep` edges are reordered wherever a random draw could invert them.
- `sides` is a float that is floored, because the bank has no `int` type.

**Usage note:** `fresnel_rim_disc` and `metal_ring_tint` are colour blocks that are black outside the shape. In the normal blend they need their matching mask (`sdf_circle` or `sdf_ring` with the same parameters), as the HD composition below shows. (12 October 2026)

## Verification, both ways (12 October 2026)

| Run | Result | Exit |
|---|---|---|
| Verifier, 35 blocks, 256 px, defaults plus 2 random sets, `--write-thumbs` | all green, worst mean 0.336, worst max 0.5 | 0 |
| Verifier, 35 blocks, 6 random sets, seed 20261012 (245 cases, 26.4 s) | all green, worst mean 0.305, worst max 0.502 | 0 |
| Full bank (37 blocks, including the 2 seeds) | all green in 14.7 s | 0 |
| Red: copy in the scratchpad (`ZRMD_SHADER_BANK`), 3 twins deliberately broken | `metal_ring_tint` (shininess halved) RED, mean 2.60, max 48.7; `ramp_oklab4` (output times 0.95) RED, mean 3.05, max 6.1 | 1 |
| `tests/test_shader_compose.py` after the harvest | green | 0 |
| 1920 x 1080 composition, 6 layers (OKLab sky, parabola glow added, masked metal ring, masked fresnel disc, specular band in screen, radial vignette in multiply), GLSL against `compose.evaluate` | mean 0.234, max 0.503 | n/a |

**Gap found in the S0 verifier.** In the red run, the broken `sdf_arc` twin (span widened by 30 degrees) stayed GREEN with a mean of 1.76 levels, even though its maximum was 255. The verifier only checks the mean error. A geometric error confined to a small area therefore passes below 2 levels.

**Proposed fix for the S0 owner (not applied).** The S0 verifier is shared with the agent writing the other families, so it was left untouched. The fix is to add a second criterion, for example `max_abs <= 4` or a share of pixels above 8 levels under 0.1 percent. Every green block measured here has a maximum of 0.502 or less, so this criterion would not turn any of them red. (12 October 2026)

